  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They held the lead until they didn't, and the bullpen couldn't save them late.",
    "what_this_means": "Mason Barnett gave them five innings of usable ball. Four hits, three runs, six strikeouts. The kind of start that should feel like enough. It wasn't. The offense did its part early, scoring in the third and fourth innings to build a three-run lead. Lawrence Butler and Jacob Wilson each had two hits and drove in a run. But baseball doesn't reward shows of force; it rewards what you finish with. Luis Medina entered in the sixth with runners still on base and the lead fragile. He gave up two earned runs in one inning and took the loss. By the eighth inning, Boston had turned the game completely over. The Athletics led after seven. They trailed after eight. That's the distance between a win and a loss when you're only winning by finding what you can grab. Three runs and a four-run loss leave nothing to remember except what slipped away.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is inconsistency wearing a box score. Nine hits and eight strikeouts is an offensive game with no conclusion. The pitching held enough of the game to keep them close. The bullpen couldn't hold the rest. You don't lose close games on singular failures. You lose them on moments that compound. Barnett's start, Butler's run production, even Scott Blewett's two clean innings: none of it mattered because one reliever couldn't get through one inning without the game flipping. This team doesn't have margin for that kind of waste. They're too thin. Two runs from one pitcher in one frame is not supposed to end everything, but for a 45-64 team chasing against a contender, it does. One pitch in the wrong place and three months of grinding disappears into August.",
